sec.p:
The P-vector method and the thermal wind relation are applied to obtain the AGC velocity from the Argo data following Chu21–23 and Yuan et al.24. The thermal wind vector formulas are as follows:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}=\frac{g}{\rho f}\frac{\partial \rho }{\partial y}$$\end{document}∂u∂z=gρf∂ρ∂y2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\frac{\partial v}{\partial z}=-\,\frac{g}{\rho f}\frac{\partial \rho }{\partial x}$$\end{document}∂v∂z=−gρf∂ρ∂xwhere u and v are the zonal and meridional velocities, respectively; x, y, and z are the zonal, meridional, and vertical coordinates, respectively; ρ is seawater density; g is gravity; and f is the Coriolis parameter.

The P-vector method is based on the conservations of potential density and potential vorticity and on two approximations of the geostrophic balance and the Boussinesq approximation. As a result, a unit vector P can be defined as:3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\rm{P}}=\frac{\nabla {\rm{\rho }}\times \nabla {\rm{q}}}{|\nabla {\rm{\rho }}\times \nabla {\rm{q}}|},$$\end{document}P=∇ρ×∇q|∇ρ×∇q|,which is the intersection of isopycnal and equal potential vorticity surfaces, where ρ is seawater density and q is potential vorticity. The AGC is assumed to follow the unit vector P, that is,4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\rm{V}}=r(x,y,z)P$$\end{document}V=r(x,y,z)Pwhere r is the proportionality coefficient. The thermal wind relation at two depths can determine r.
